Q: Redirecting STDERR output using Perl and exec A: <p>Your approach is so complex. Using IPC::Run:</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>use IPC::Run qw( run );

run [ 'zombieWriter', $plc ], '&gt;', 'stdout.txt', '2&gt;&amp;1';
</code></pre>

Q: How does one initialize the bizarre Vulkan &quot;const char* const* ppEnabledLayerNames;&quot; inside VkInstanceCreateInfo using C A: <p>A constant variable (pointer or otherwise) is a variable that can't be change directly after initialization.</p>
<p>Given <code>const char * const * buf</code> aka <code>char const * const * buf</code>, <code>buf</code> is a pointer to a constant pointer to a constant <code>char</code> object. (Read it backwards.) Therefore,</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>char const * const buf0 = &quot;...&quot;;
char const * const buf1 = &quot;...&quot;;

*buf0 = '.';   // Error. The char object is constant
buf0 = &quot;...&quot;;  // Error. The pointer is constant.

char const * const * p = &amp;buf0;
p = &amp;buf1;     // Ok. `p` isn't constant.
</code></pre>

Q: How to safely wait for new data in a gRPC C++ server streaming reactor using the Callback API? A: <p>I had a need to do this as well and the rough outline Mark gave in his answer was quite helpful. However, you need some additional fields and logic to handle cancellation. I'll share what I ended up with, but note that I haven't extensively tested this in production, though from what I can tell it appears to work and respects cancellation.</p>
<p>My <code>MaybeStartWrite</code> function looks a bit more like a state machine transition:</p>
<pre><code>void LogReactor::MaybeStartWrite() {
  std::lock_guard lock{m_mutex};

  if (m_writeInProgress) {
    return;
  }

  if (m_writeQueue.empty()) {
    if (m_dataComplete) {
      const grpc::Status status =
          m_cancelled ? grpc::Status::CANCELLED : grpc::Status::OK;

      this-&gt;Finish(status);
    }

    if (m_error) {
      this-&gt;Finish(grpc::Status(grpc::StatusCode::INTERNAL, &quot;&quot;));
    }

    return;
  }

  m_currentMessage = std::move(m_writeQueue.front());
  m_writeQueue.pop();

  StartWrite(&amp;m_currentMessage);

  m_writeInProgress = true;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I have quite a few boolean flags that contribute to the 'state' of the reactor:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>m_writeInProgress</code>: as mentioned in the earlier answer, flipped to true before calling <code>StartWrite</code> and flipped to false when in <code>OnWriteDone</code>.</li>
<li><code>m_dataComplete</code>: exposes a setter and provides a way for the producing thread to signal that no more data is coming. If there is no more data coming and the write queue is empty, then we can call <code>Finish</code>.</li>
<li><code>m_error</code>: has a setter and allows the producing thread to signal an error occurred. I use this in an exception handler so for me it also implies <code>m_dataComplete</code> as the producer has thrown an exception and will not be queuing up any more writes.</li>
<li><code>m_cancelled</code>: set in <code>OnCancel</code> and indicates that the RPC has been cancelled by the client. This is surfaced via a getter.</li>
</ul>
<p>The other notable member variable is <code>m_currentMessage</code>, which I only have because I am scared of the <code>std::queue</code> reallocating if another thread adds an element after I have just passed <code>StartWrite</code> a pointer to its first element. I don't know if that's actually superfluous and the spec actually guarantees that can't happen.</p>
<p>Note that with regards to cancellation, my <code>OnCancel</code> doesn't call <code>Finish</code>. This is because I wanted the producer end to explicitly have to acknowledge cancellation and stop producing data, because:</p>
<ul>
<li>I want to avoid further writes racing with <code>Finish</code> (which is called out as being
illegal in the gRPC C++ best practices doc).</li>
<li>Or, worse yet, <code>Finish</code> leads to <code>OnDone</code> being called and then the reactor calls <code>delete this;</code> and the producer thread attempts a use-after-free.</li>
</ul>
<p>The producer should check the cancellation status via the getter and, if cancelled, abort any further writes and set <code>m_dataComplete</code>, which will then be reacted to in <code>MaybeStartWrite()</code>. The setters for these flags also call <code>MaybeStartWrite()</code> to evaluate if <code>Finish</code> needs to be called:</p>
<pre><code>void LogReactor::SetDataComplete() {
  {
    std::lock_guard lock{m_mutex};
    m_dataComplete = true;
  }

  // Evaluate if we need to call Finish: if we call this after all writes have
  // completed, then our change to m_dataComplete will do nothing
  MaybeStartWrite();
}

void LogReactor::SetError() {
  {
    std::lock_guard lock{m_mutex};
    m_error = true;
  }

  MaybeStartWrite();
}
</code></pre>
<p>My producer end is a fairly simple libpqxx stream which takes a pointer to the reactor and writes logs as they are streamed from the database:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void DoCallbackExport(LogReactor *reactor) {
  pqxx::connection cx = CreatePostgresConnection();
  pqxx::read_transaction tx = pqxx::read_transaction(cx);

  auto stream = tx.stream&lt;std::int32_t, double, std::string&gt;(Query);

  for (const auto &amp;[id, timestamp, message] : stream) {
    proto::Log protoMessage{};
    protoMessage.set_message(std::format(&quot;{},{},\&quot;{}\&quot;&quot;, id, timestamp, message));

    if (reactor-&gt;IsCancelled()) {
      break;
    }

    reactor-&gt;EnqueueWrite(protoMessage);
    reactor-&gt;MaybeStartWrite();
  }

  reactor-&gt;SetDataComplete();

  tx.commit();
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>EnqueueWrite</code> does what it says on the tin and just adds a message to the write queue.</p>
<p>The last piece of the puzzle is on what thread the producer runs. As the question notes, doing these sorts of I/O-bound tasks within the gRPC thread pool generally seems to be discouraged. I can think of two approaches at the minute: one would be to run the work in a thread pool, kicking it off with a pointer to the reactor after it is created, and the other is to let the reactor own a <code>std::jthread</code> which is the producer. This would have to be surfaced via a setter, as you have a circular dependency otherwise in which the reactor needs a <code>jthread</code> and the <code>jthread</code> needs the reactor.</p>
<p>The second option has the advantage of allowing you to control cancellation more directly, as your <code>OnCancel</code> can signal <code>request_stop()</code> on the producer then <code>join()</code> the thread to guarantee no more writes will be made. This is technically blocking but should be relatively lightweight if your code is good about checking <code>stop_requested()</code> regularly (and it is nice to be able to use <code>std::stop_token</code> rather than arbitrary methods on a reactor). That said, even though this gives you further guarantees, I still don't think you should call <code>Finish</code> right then and there because the previous write could be in progress.</p>

Q: 3D object rotation issue A: <p>You’re seeing this because rotations are order-dependent. Once you rotate around X, the object’s local Y axis tilts into Z. That’s why your &quot;Y rotation&quot; looks like Z. The fix is to use quaternions or rebuild the matrix from angles each frame so rotations stay intuitive.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void OpenGlWidget::paintGL()
{
    glClear(GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT | GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT);
    glBindTexture(GL_TEXTURE_2D, mPushButton-&gt;texture);
    glBindVertexArray(mPushButton-&gt;vao);
    glm::quat qx = glm::angleAxis(
        glm::radians(m_rotationAngleX), glm::vec3(1,0,0));
    glm::quat qy = glm::angleAxis(glm::radians(m_rotationAngleY),
        glm::vec3(0,1,0));
    glm::mat4 model = glm::toMat4(qy * qx);
    glUniformMatrix4fv(glGetUniformLocation(m_shaderProgram, &quot;model&quot;),
        1, GL_FALSE, glm::value_ptr(model));
    glDrawElements(GL_TRIANGLES, mPushButton-&gt;indices.size(),
        GL_UNSIGNED_INT, 0);
    glBindVertexArray(0);
}
</code></pre>
